 "_conventions": {
  "frame": "deck frame: origin = deck body (skateboard) origin = centre of the flat deck slab; board level on its wheels at rest -> deck origin z 0.022, deck top 0.024. pos = trunk_base free-joint position: x, y relative to the deck origin, z absolute above the ground.",
  "rpy": "trunk_base orientation relative to the deck heading, R = Rz(yaw) @ Ry(pitch) @ Rx(roll) (intrinsic ZYX); yaw +pi/2 -> robot +x = deck +y (rider faces deck +y, LEFT sole at deck -x, RIGHT sole at deck +x).",
  "joints": "10 leg joints in radians (robot joint names, no prefix); head/neck at HOME.",
  "model": "get_skate_robot_spec (groundcontact + {left,right}_sole_deck_collision boxes 45.6 x 34 mm); sole_deck_xy = box-sole bottom centres in the deck frame.",
  "regular": "soles at deck x -+0.045 (box 34 mm along the deck: spans 0.028..0.062, covers the axles at 0.055; the toed-out corner stays 2.5 mm short of the kick bend at 0.067), y -0.004 (4 mm toward the heels / deck -y), toes out ~7 deg (hip yaw), trunk upright, robot CoM over the deck centre. Wider flat-sole stances do not fit: with no ankle roll the sole can only stay flat with hip roll at HOME, the lateral reach comes from hip yaw (toe-out), and a toed-out sole then overlaps the kick bend (+-0.046 is the widest flat fit). See FEASIBILITY.md P1/P2.",
  "regular_y0": "as regular with the soles centred on the deck (y 0): toe and heel support margins about equal (the toed-out soles' support spans about -23..+24 mm vs -27..+20 mm for regular, whose toe-side lean limit (6-8 mm) binds before the heel side (10-15 mm)); hold-validated like the others (p2_results.json).",
  "mirror": "the board is symmetric: the mirrored stance (rider facing deck -y) is the same pose with board yaw + pi."
 }
